  "guidePosture": "POSTURE",
  "guidePostureDesc": "Sit on the edge of a chair or in zazen. Back straight but relaxed. Shoulders relaxed, hands in mudra (oval lock).",
  "guideGaze": "GAZE",
  "guideGazeDesc": "Eyes half-open, gaze directed downward at ~45° to the floor 1–1.5 meters ahead. Do not close your eyes — this leads to drowsiness and daydreaming.",
  "guideFocus": "FOCUS",
  "guideFocusDesc": "Blur your vision — don't stare at the floor texture, don't fixate on points. Use peripheral vision. You look but don't see details.",
  "guideAlgorithm": "Count each exhale. When you reach 10, start counting backward to 1. If a thought interrupts the count — return to one.",
  "guideBioEffect": "Biological Effect",
  "guideBioEffectDesc": "Counting engages the prefrontal cortex, blocking the brain's \"default mode network\" responsible for mind-wandering and anxiety.",
  "guideZenRule": "Zen Rule",
  "guideZenRuleQuote": "\"If you lose count at 9 — you've lost the battle for attention. Humbly return to 1. This is the practice.\"",
  "guideZazenGeometry": "Zazen Geometry",
  "guideFormContent": "Form and Content",
  "guideVertical": "Vertical",
  "guideVerticalDesc": "Spine straight like a string. This is the physiological foundation for an alert consciousness.",
  "guideGazeZazen": "Gaze",
  "guideGazeZazenDesc": "Eyes half-open, gaze at 45° down, focus soft. You don't drift into dreams, you stay here and now.",
  "guideMudra": "Mudra",
  "guideMudraDesc": "Hands in an oval lock. This is your physical sensor of concentration depth.",
